# Error Analysis

## Purpose

Beyond aggregate metrics (Precision, Recall, mAP), this analysis inspects individual wrong detections to understand *why* errors happen and what could reduce them. Errors were computed per-image using a custom IoU-matching script (`tools/error_analyzer.py`) at IoU threshold 0.5.

---

## Error Summary by Model

| Model | Test Images | Images w/ Errors | True Positives | False Positives | False Negatives |
|---|---:|---:|---:|---:|---:|
| YOLO11s | 1068 | 72 (6.7%) | 1642 | 61 | 20 |
| YOLO26n | 1068 | 175 (16.4%) | 1610 | 157 | 52 |
| YOLO26m | 1068 | 79 (7.4%) | 1640 | 65 | 22 |

YOLO26n has noticeably more errors than the other two models — both more false positives (extra/wrong detections) and more false negatives (missed defects). This is consistent with it having the lowest Precision and Recall in the official evaluation (`05_evaluation.md`), and matches expectations for the lightweight "nano" variant: fewer parameters means less capacity to distinguish subtle defect patterns from normal board texture.

YOLO11s and YOLO26m perform similarly on error counts, despite YOLO26m being trained for only 11 epochs vs YOLO11s's 20 — suggesting YOLO26m's architecture reaches strong performance faster, though longer training may close the gap further (see Future Work in `09_yolo11_vs_yolo26.md`).

---

## Confirmed Findings (from full error log analysis)

The three `results-*.txt` files were parsed in full — every individual false positive and false negative, across all three models, categorized by defect class and confidence score.

### 1. Spurious Copper and Short dominate false positives, consistently across all three models

| Model | Top FP classes (count) |
|---|---|
| YOLO11s | Short (21), Spurious Copper (14), Mouse Bite (11), Missing Hole (7) |
| YOLO26n | Spurious Copper (50), Short (38), Spur (25), Missing Hole (19) |
| YOLO26m | Spurious Copper (19), Short (13), Mouse Bite (12), Missing Hole (10) |

Spurious Copper and Short are the two most over-predicted classes in every single model, not just one. This is a strong, repeatable signal — not noise. Both defect types are visually defined by *irregular copper-colored shapes/traces on the board*, which is exactly the kind of pattern that can appear in normal board texture, solder traces, and copper pours. The model is very likely picking up on ordinary copper features and misreading them as defects.

### 2. Spur is the most consistently missed defect (false negative) across all models

| Model | Top FN classes (count) |
|---|---|
| YOLO11s | Spur (7), Spurious Copper (5), Open Circuit (5) |
| YOLO26n | Spurious Copper (13), Spur (12), Short (11) |
| YOLO26m | Spur (8), Spurious Copper (5), Short (4) |

Spur appears as a top-2 missed class in every model. Spur defects are thin, sharp protrusions — among the smallest and most geometrically subtle defect types in this dataset, which fits the small-object-detection difficulty already documented in the mAP50 vs mAP50-95 gap.

### 3. Most false positives are near-hallucinations, not localization errors

Across all three models, the overwhelming majority of false positives have `best_iou = 0.00` — meaning the model detected a defect in a location with **no ground truth defect at all**, not a defect it found in roughly the right place but boxed imprecisely. Only 2–5 false positives per model (out of 61–157) had any partial box overlap. This matters: it means the primary false-positive problem is the model **imagining defects in clean regions**, not misplacing boxes around real ones. That's a different failure mode than the mAP50/50-95 localization-precision gap, and needs a different fix (see Recommendations).

### 4. YOLO26n's false positives are disproportionately low-confidence

| Model | FP mean confidence | FP with confidence < 0.5 |
|---|---|---|
| YOLO11s | 0.51 | 44% |
| YOLO26n | 0.43 | **73%** |
| YOLO26m | 0.54 | 46% |

YOLO26n's false positives skew heavily toward low confidence — nearly three-quarters sit below 0.5. This is actionable: a higher confidence threshold at inference specifically benefits YOLO26n, likely filtering out a large share of its false positives with comparatively little cost to recall, since genuine defects tend to be detected with higher confidence.

### 5. Certain images fail for every model, regardless of architecture

Some file names appear as errors in all three logs — e.g. `l_light_04_spur_05_2_600.jpg` (missed Spur in all three), `rotation_270_light_01_short_18_1_600.jpg` (Short false positive + false negative in all three), `rotation_270_light_01_open_circuit_13_3_600.jpg` (missed Open Circuit in all three). These are architecture-independent failures — the issue lives in the image/label itself (genuinely hard example, ambiguous ground truth, or an image where the defect is barely visible even to a human), not in any one model's weaknesses. Worth manually opening these specific files to check if the ground truth annotation itself is accurate.

---

## Recommendations to Reduce These Errors

Ranked by expected impact, directly tied to the confirmed findings above:

1. **Raise the inference confidence threshold, especially for YOLO26n.** Finding #4 shows 73% of YOLO26n's false positives sit below 0.5 confidence — this is close to a free fix. Check the F1 curve in `05_evaluation.md` to pick a threshold that maximizes F1, then re-run `tools/error_analyzer.py` at that new threshold to confirm the false-positive count actually drops without meaningfully hurting recall.

2. **Add hard-negative training examples for Spurious Copper and Short.** Finding #1 shows these two classes are over-predicted in clean/normal board regions across all three models — a consistent, architecture-independent pattern. This suggests the model needs more exposure to normal copper traces and solder regions *without* a defect label, so it learns the visual boundary between "normal copper feature" and "actual defect" more precisely. Standard augmentation (mosaic, flip, HSV) won't fix this — the issue is what's already correctly labeled as *background*.

3. **Investigate Spur detection specifically.** Finding #2 shows Spur is the most consistently missed class. Combined with it being a small, thin defect type, this points to either: (a) increasing input resolution (`imgsz` above 640) so thin defects retain more pixels, or (b) reviewing whether Spur has visibly fewer or lower-quality training examples than other classes.

4. **Manually review the cross-model failure images.** Finding #5 identified specific images that fail for every model regardless of architecture (e.g. `l_light_04_spur_05_2_600.jpg`, `rotation_270_light_01_short_18_1_600.jpg`). Since the failure isn't architecture-specific, check these images and their ground truth labels directly — this may reveal an annotation quality issue, not a model weakness. Worth doing before investing more training time.

5. **Not a false-positive localization problem.** Finding #3 shows almost no false positives have partial box overlap (near-zero IoU in nearly all cases) — meaning tightening NMS or IoU-matching logic won't help here. The false positives are hallucinated detections in the wrong location entirely, which is why recommendation #2 (better negative examples) matters more than post-processing tuning.

